{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_71","type":"reasoning","text":"Now I'll write report.md. Need to keep it under 500 words. Draft (approx. 420 words):\n\n---\n\n# Differential Gene Expression: Combination Treatment with Cisplatin + CBD vs. DMSO\n\n## Objective and Design\nUsing PyDESeq2 0.5.0, I compared cells treated with the combination of cisplatin and CBD at IC50 (Cisplatin_IC50_CBD_IC50, samples 9-1/9-2/9-3) against DMSO-treated controls (3-1/3-2/3-3), with log2 fold changes expressed as combination treatment relative to DMSO. Samples from the other 8 experimental groups were excluded. The design used a single term (`~condition`, i.e., Group).\n\n## Filtering and Methods\nFrom 63,677 raw Ensembl genes, I retained the 18,029 genes with raw counts exceeding 10 in at least one of the 6 samples, and then ran the full DESeq2 pipeline (median-of-ratios normalization, gene-wise/trend/MAP dispersion estimation, Wald test, `refit_cooks=True`). The standard `DeseqStats` contrast `[\"condition\", \"Cisplatin_IC50_CBD_IC50\", \"DMSO\"]` was used. Due to DESeq2's independent filtering, 4,196 genes do not have an adjusted p-value, and these are recorded as null (empty cells in the CSV, `null` in JSON), not as zero.\n\n## Results\nOut of the 18,029 genes tested, **557** met the criteria padj < 0.05, |log2FC| > 0.5, baseMean > 10: **359 up-regulated** and **198 down-regulated** genes in the combination treatment.\n\nStrongly up-regulated genes include the following:\n- CDKN1A (p21; log2FC ≈ 1.20) — a DNA damage response consistent with cisplatin-induced genotoxic stress.\n- CYP1A1 (≈2.03) and AHRR (≈2.13) — xenobiotic/aryl hydrocarbon receptor pathway genes, consistent with exposure to CBD.\n- ALDH1A3 (≈1.62), GDA (≈1.28), NR2F2, SMAD3, FDXR.\nThe most strongly down-regulated gene is ANKRD37 (log2FC ≈ −2.0).\n\nThe full table is in `differential_expression.csv` (keyed by Ensembl ID, with gene symbols attached). Counts are in `summary.json`. The reproducible script is `analysis.py`.\n\n## Reproducibility Note\nThe installed anndata 0.13.3 automatically converts 1-dimensional arrays stored in `varm`/`obsm` to (n,1), which breaks PyDESeq2 0.5.0. `analysis.py` applies a minimal, documented compatibility shim that restores the conventional 1-dimensional behavior, and no other part of the library is modified.\n\n## Association vs. Causation\nThese results measure **statistical association**: the expression differences are associated with the treatment group assignment in this cell line experiment. They do not establish that the listed genes **cause** the sensitivity or resistance to the combination treatment. A treatment-induced change in mRNA level may be a downstream consequence of stress, cell cycle arrest, or cell population composition changes, rather than a mediator of drug response. Conversely, causal regulators may not change their own expression. Establishing causality requires perturbation experiments (knockdown/overexpression, dose response, rescue assays) and validation in independent models. In addition, since the 6 samples were processed in a single batch, batch effects cannot be separated from treatment effects here.\n\n---\n\nRough word count — estimating around 430 words. Good.\n\nAfter that, final consolidated artifact check and cleanup of scratch files."}}
